  "response": "<solution>\nimport pandas as pd\nimport sqlite3\n\ndef task_func(db_path: str, table_name: str, column_name: str) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    \"\"\"\n    Loads data from an SQLite database into a Pandas DataFrame and replaces all occurrences \n    of the newline character '\\n' with the HTML line break tag '<br>' in a specified column.\n    \n    Args:\n        db_path (str): The path to the SQLite database.\n        table_name (str): The name of the table to read.\n        column_name (str): The name of the column to perform the replacement on.\n        \n    Returns:\n        pd.DataFrame: The modified DataFrame.\n    \"\"\"\n    conn = sqlite3.connect(db_path)\n    try:\n        # Properly escape double quotes in the table name to prevent SQL injection or syntax errors\n        safe_table_name = table_name.replace('\"', '\"\"')\n        query = f'SELECT * FROM \"{safe_table_name}\"'\n        df = pd.read_sql_query(query, conn)\n    finally:\n        conn.close()\n        \n    if column_name not in df.columns:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Column '{column_name}' does not exist in table '{table_name}'.\")\n        \n    # Using apply with isinstance safely handles mixed data types and NaNs without unintended conversions\n    df[column_name] = df[column_name].apply(\n        lambda x: x.replace('\\n', '<br>') if isinstance(x, str) else x\n    )\n    \n    return df\n</solution>\n\nConfidence level: High. The solution robustly uses raw `sqlite3` queries properly escaping the table name, cleanly manages the database connection lifecycle, and safely targets only strings within the dataframe column using `apply` to naturally side-step any missing (`NaN`/`None`) or non-string values.",
